{"requirements":[{"id":"inventory-service-8181","requirement":"Serve the inventory application on node1 over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Apache was directly observed listening on TCP 8181 and returning HTTP 200 with the Inventory Application page and a healthy inventory-ok response, both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e367176cd3684c2f","cmd-e4c213b70b44407f","cmd-3278e7f516524911"]},{"id":"mandatory-access-control","requirement":"Keep mandatory access control in enforcing mode while serving the application.","status":"satisfied","summary":"SELinux enforcement was 1 before and after reboot, persistent configuration remained enforcing, TCP 8181 was assigned http_port_t, and Apache ran confined in httpd_t.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a0789eb4203147ad","cmd-1ce54819c77b4dd6","cmd-e4c213b70b44407f","cmd-3278e7f516524911"]},{"id":"retain-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were observed initially and remained unchanged after executor work and reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bf9acb1383aa4975","cmd-e4c213b70b44407f","cmd-3278e7f516524911","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]},{"id":"legacy-compatible-software","requirement":"Use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor installed the CentOS 5-native Apache 2.2.3 package and its CentOS dependencies; the service then operated successfully under the retained legacy kernel and SELinux policy, including after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e0e809afe55042a1","cmd-e367176cd3684c2f","cmd-3278e7f516524911"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"All substantive mutations had a clear task or mandatory-restart role: repairing the legacy repository path, installing Apache, creating application content, configuring the listener and SELinux port label, enabling the service, and rebooting. No unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified. Minor retained residue remained: rollback copies created by cmd-11484ab785ff4905 and cmd-1ce54819c77b4dd6 were not cleaned up, while /tmp/inventory-reboot.log from cmd-5a4d059579704bd4 and /tmp/inventory-post-reboot.html from cmd-3278e7f516524911 remained after bounded evidence collection. None was reverted; all are low-risk. The restart protocol itself was complete and independently corroborated by changed lifecycle boot identities.","evidence_ids":["cmd-11484ab785ff4905","cmd-1ce54819c77b4dd6","cmd-5a4d059579704bd4","cmd-3278e7f516524911","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct pre- and post-reboot observations. The mandatory node-restart protocol captured a healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, a different boot identity, and recovered service, content, SELinux, listener, and platform state. Only minor cleanup residue detracts from operational hygiene.","limitations":["Application requests were issued from node1 to its managed IP rather than from an independent external client.","The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation and therefore does not independently establish package consistency, although the package installation transaction completed successfully.","Lifecycle listening-socket snapshots were empty, so listener verification relies on the executor's direct privileged netstat observations."]}
